{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Hark good traveler of far-flung British isle\nLet me weave thee an itinerary most beguiling\nIn fair Japan where cherry blossoms do twirl\n\nMark the first morn in Tokyo where the sun doth rise\nBehold the torii gates of Fushimi Inari’s trail\nA thousand vermillion gates in sacred guise\nDo climb with zeal lest thee they to heaven enthrall\n\nOn to Asakusa where Senso-ji stands tall\nIncense thick like London fog on a dreary day\nPurchase a fortune from a monk with eyes so small\nThen taste the melon pan sweet as a lover’s sway\n\nIn Shinjuku let thy feet in neon glow\nOmoide Yokocho’s alleys where the lanterns gleam\nDine on yakitori sizzling like a bard’s last show\nThen lose thyself in Golden Gai where secrets steam\n\nLo the second morn shall find thee in Kyoto\nWhere temples whisper secrets old as time’s own thread\nKinkaku-ji’s pavilion gleams like a king’s own gold\nWhile Arashiyama’s bamboo doth the wind have led\n\nTo Nara next where deer in freedom do roam\nBow before them as thou wouldst a noble lord\nThen at Todai-ji view the giant’s bronze form\nThe Great Buddha’s eyes upon thy actions pour\n\nLet the third day transport thee to Osaka’s embrace\nDotonbori’s lights a kaleidoscope of cheer\nStreet food of every form shall tempt thy taste\nTakoyaki balls like moons upon a summer’s night appear\n\nBehold the fourth morn on Mt. Fuji’s grandeur\nShould the clouds part clear as a maiden’s prayer\nOr if their veil doth shroud the peak’s proud frame\nThen to Hakone where onsens do declare\n\nImmerse thyself in baths of mineral grace\nLet thy cares dissolve like dew in morning’s beam\nThen dine on black eggs a delicacy most strange\nAnd feast on kuro tamago with a hearty dream\n\nOn the fifth day let thy ship or train thee bear\nTo Hiroshima where peace doth solemnly reside\nAt the Park of Memory stand and bide awhile\nThen to Miyajima w